"""Synchronize accepted prose only; preserve equations, tables and figures."""
from pathlib import Path
from datetime import datetime
import re,json,subprocess,hashlib
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup
ROOT=next(p for p in Path(__file__).resolve().parents if (p/'AGENTS.md').exists())
HERE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
P=ROOT/'dlw-paper.html';raw=P.read_bytes();original=raw.decode('utf-8');doc=original
backup=HERE/('before_'+datetime.now().strftime('%Y%m%d_%H%M%S')+'.html');backup.write_bytes(raw)
maths=[];edits=[]
def para(s):
    bits=s.split('$');assert len(bits)%2==1
    out=[]
    for i,t in enumerate(bits):
        if i%2:maths.append(t);out.append(f'ACCEPTEDMATH{len(maths)-1}END')
        else:out.append(t)
    return '<p>'+''.join(out)+'</p>'
def pars(*ss):return '\n'.join(para(s) for s in ss)
def change(sid,indices,replacement):
    global doc
    start=re.search(r'<section\b[^>]*\bid="'+re.escape(sid)+'"',doc).start();end=doc.index('</section>',start)
    section=doc[start:end];matches=list(re.finditer(r'<p>[\s\S]*?</p>',section))
    inds=[indices] if isinstance(indices,int) else list(indices)
    inds=[i if i>=0 else len(matches)+i for i in inds]
    for i in sorted(inds,reverse=True):
        m=matches[i];section=section[:m.start()]+(replacement if i==min(inds) else '')+section[m.end():]
    doc=doc[:start]+section+doc[end:];edits.append([sid,inds])

change('section-1',range(6),pars(
'The propagation and interaction of shallow-water waves have long attracted interest in the study of nonlinear wave phenomena. Nonlinearity and dispersion shape the evolving wave profiles, and their balance can support coherent waves that persist during propagation. Nonlinear long-wave equations describe these effects in a reduced form, allowing wave motion to be studied without retaining the full complexity of the underlying fluid equations. Within this class, integrable models provide exact solutions through which individual waves and their interactions can be examined, together with bilinear formulations and Lax representations that describe the structures governing their evolution. These analytical results also provide reference solutions for numerical computation. Representing the waves on a spatial lattice introduces choices that affect both their approximation and the structure of the governing equations. Integrable discretization brings these questions together by constructing discrete equations alongside their exact solutions and continuum limits.',
'The $(2+1)$-dimensional dispersive long-wave (DLW) system extends the dispersive long-wave equation to two spatial dimensions [1] and has been studied in connection with wave motion in wide channels and open seas of finite depth [3,4]. Since its introduction by Boiti, Leon and Pempinelli, its solutions have been investigated through several related analytical approaches. The inverse spectral transform, Bäcklund transformation and superposition formula established in the original work [1] were followed by nonlinear transformations [2] and bilinear methods [3], providing further ways to construct wave solutions and understand their relations. Determinant representations give these solution families an explicit form. Hu, Xu and Yu developed Grammian and Wronskian solutions [4], while Sheng and Yu used a KP-hierarchy reduction to obtain Gram determinant solutions describing solitons, line breathers and rational waves on constant and periodic backgrounds [5]. Their bilinear formulation and determinant construction provide the continuous starting point for the present study.',
'The bilinear relations underlying these solutions also offer a starting point for introducing a spatial lattice [15]. Bäcklund transformations can relate neighbouring lattice values, while expansions of the Hirota–Miwa equation connect discrete bilinear relations to equations with continuous variables. These approaches have been developed for semi-discrete soliton equations [7] and extended to systems with two spatial dimensions [8]. For the DLW system, a further connection comes from its bilinear relation to the $(2+1)$-dimensional sinh-Gordon equation [5], whose integrable discretizations admit determinant solutions and Bäcklund–Lax structures [6]. Together with earlier studies of discrete DLW systems [11], these developments provide a basis for carrying continuous wave families onto a spatial lattice. They also invite a closer examination of what this passage means for the physical waves, whose approximation depends on both the lattice equations and the reconstruction of the physical fields.',
'Building on this work, we wish to understand how the preservation of integrable structure can contribute to the approximation and computation of continuous waves. We seek a semi-discrete setting in which the bilinear equations, exact solutions, and reconstructed physical fields can be studied together, allowing us to trace the effect of introducing a lattice from the analytical construction to the resulting wave profiles. In particular, we ask how different lattice arrangements within a common integrable framework affect the accuracy of the physical fields, and how the available analytical structure can guide their numerical computation. These questions connect the construction of integrable lattices with the broader task of understanding and improving numerical approximations of nonlinear waves.',
'To investigate these questions, we construct two integrable semi-discretizations of the DLW system from the Hirota–Miwa equation. The endpoint construction, denoted by Endpoint-SD, follows from a Miwa expansion. Starting from this lattice, we introduce a shift of the parameter reference and a staggered placement of the $\\tau$ functions to obtain the centered construction, denoted by Centered-SD. We establish their Gram determinant solutions and Darboux–Lax representations, and show how the lattice arrangement enters the reconstructed physical-field equations. Endpoint-SD has second-order consistency in the first equation and first-order consistency in the second, whereas Centered-SD achieves second-order consistency in both. We then establish the convergence orders of the corresponding exact wave fields and derive their leading error coefficients under the stated assumptions. These results show how two constructions with shared integrable structures can produce different approximation properties, and identify the role of lattice placement and field reconstruction in determining those properties.',
'The exact solutions provide benchmarks for examining how the analytical approximation properties are reflected in numerical computation. We use spatial refinement tests to assess the predicted orders and follow the physical-field errors through time on soliton, Gaussian, and sinusoidal backgrounds. These experiments, together with the spatial error analysis, help explain how the formulation and time integrator affect the accuracy of the evolving waves. The semi-discrete conservation law also supports a shared-node moving mesh, which reduces the errors in both fields at a fixed node count in the soliton tests. Independent initial perturbations extend the study to the development of coupled responses within each formulation. Through these investigations, we seek to clarify how the analytical structure of an integrable semi-discretization can help us understand numerical behavior and improve the computation of nonlinear waves.',
'The paper is organized as follows. Section 2 introduces the continuous DLW bilinear system and develops the Endpoint-SD and Centered-SD constructions, together with their Gram determinant solutions and positivity conditions. Section 3 reconstructs the nonlinear physical-field equations, establishes their Darboux–Lax representations, and examines the continuum limits of the equations and exact wave fields. Building on these formulations, Section 4 describes the spatial discretization, initial and boundary data, time integration, and conservation-based moving mesh. Section 5 presents the numerical experiments on physical-field accuracy, mesh adaptation, and the evolution of independent initial perturbations. Section 6 summarizes the main findings.'))
change('section-4',0,para('In this section, we develop numerical formulations for computing the physical fields introduced above. The semi-discrete equations retain continuous dependence on $x$ and $t$, so their numerical evolution requires a further spatial approximation, initial and boundary data, and a time-integration scheme. We specify these ingredients for Endpoint-SD and Centered-SD, together with a direct finite-difference discretization of the continuous DLW system as a baseline. Section 4.1 introduces the common differentiation operators in $x$ and the resulting spatial formulations. Section 4.2 specifies the initial and boundary data and describes how the fields are advanced in time. In Section 4.3, we use the Centered-SD conservation law to construct a shared moving mesh, allowing the longitudinal resolution to adapt to the evolving fields.'))
change('section-4.1',0,para('We first discretize the remaining $x$ derivatives to obtain systems that can be advanced in time. Endpoint-SD, Centered-SD, and the direct finite-difference baseline use the same Fourier differentiation in $x$, providing a common longitudinal approximation for examining their different transverse formulations. Denote the numerical first- and second-derivative operators by $\\mathcal D_x$ and $\\mathcal D_{xx}$, with Fourier multipliers $\\mathrm i\\xi$ and $-\\xi^2$, respectively. The first-derivative multiplier is set to zero at the Nyquist frequency, and nonlinear products are evaluated at the grid points. The calligraphic notation distinguishes these numerical operators from Hirota’s bilinear derivatives.'))
change('section-4.1',-1,para('The three spatial formulations use the same Fourier differentiation in $x$ and the prescribed lower-boundary data for recovering the $u$ time derivative. Their different approximations enter through the transverse differences, nonlinear fluxes, and placement of the physical fields. This common numerical setting allows us to examine how the endpoint and centered lattice constructions affect the computed waves, with direct finite differences providing a reference discretization of the continuous equations. It also connects the consistency analysis of Section 3 with the spatial errors measured in the numerical experiments.'))
change('section-4.2',0,para('In this subsection, we specify the initial and boundary data and describe how the resulting spatial systems are advanced in time. The initial states are obtained by sampling the same continuous reference fields at the native locations of each formulation. Endpoint-SD and FD advance $z=(u_k,v_k)_k$, while Centered-SD advances $z=(u_k,\\omega_k)_k$ and reconstructs $v$ through the relation in Section 4.1. At each stage, the current state and the prescribed boundary data supply the time derivatives required by the integrator. We use forward Euler, classical RK4, and Crank–Nicolson to examine how different temporal approximations affect the computed physical fields.'))
# Preserve the implicit iteration and tolerance-control text verbatim.
st=re.search(r'<section\b[^>]*\bid="section-4\.2"',doc).start();en=doc.index('</section>',st)
last=list(re.finditer(r'<p>[\s\S]*?</p>',doc[st:en]))[-1][0]
prefix=last[:last.index(' The spatial comparisons in Section 5.2')]+ '</p>'
change('section-4.2',-1,prefix+'\n'+para('The spatial and temporal comparisons in Sections 5.2 and 5.3 use $\\Delta t=10^{-4}$ and $T=0.01$; the mesh experiments in Section 5.4 use RK4 with the same step size and final time. In each comparison, the advanced state is expressed in terms of the physical fields $u$ and $v$ for error evaluation. This provides a common basis for assessing the numerical evolution across formulations with different state variables. With the spatial operators and boundary treatment fixed, the time-integrator comparisons then allow us to follow how the temporal approximation influences the accumulation of errors in the two coupled fields.'))
change('section-4.3',0,para('The spatial variation of a wave is often concentrated in a small part of the computational domain. A uniform grid distributes nodes equally across these regions and the more slowly varying background, while an adaptive mesh can allocate additional resolution where the fields vary more strongly. In this subsection, we first establish a semi-discrete conservation law for Centered-SD and then use its density and flux to construct a self-adaptive moving mesh. The nodes move in $x$, while the $y$ lattice remains uniform and all transverse layers share the same longitudinal mesh. This arrangement retains the transverse lattice pairings and allows the distribution of resolution in $x$ to evolve with the physical fields.'))
change('section-4.3',1,para('To establish the conservation law, we introduce the density $\\rho_k=1-\\omega_k/h$ and define the associated flux $q_k$ by'))
change('section-4.3',2,para('Substituting $\\omega_k=h(1-\\rho_k)$ into the auxiliary equation of Centered-SD gives $\\partial_t\\rho_k+\\partial_xq_k=0$. We choose the additive constant in $q_k$ so that the flux vanishes on the zero-field background. This conservation relation connects the evolution of the density to its spatial transport and forms the basis of the mesh construction below.'))
st=re.search(r'<section\b[^>]*\bid="section-4\.3"',doc).start();en=doc.index('</section>',st);last=list(re.finditer(r'<p>[\s\S]*?</p>',doc[st:en]))[-1][0]
change('section-4.3',-1,last+'\n'+para('The resulting scheme couples the physical-field evolution to a mesh determined by the semi-discrete conservation law. The averaged density specifies the initial distribution of nodes, and the associated flux governs their subsequent motion through the preservation of cumulative-density labels. By sharing the longitudinal nodes across all transverse layers, the construction accommodates mesh adaptation within the existing Centered-SD field reconstruction. It thus provides a concrete way to use the analytical structure of the equations to guide spatial resolution, whose effect on the computed wave fields can be assessed at a fixed node count.'))
change('section-5',0,para('In this section, we examine how the semi-discrete formulations developed above perform in numerical computation. Our focus is on the accuracy of the evolving physical fields and the roles of spatial discretization, time integration, and mesh distribution in determining that accuracy. We also follow the response to independent initial perturbations to examine how disturbances develop through the coupling of the two fields. Section 5.1 introduces the test problems, continuous reference solutions, and error measures. Section 5.2 examines the spatial approximations and compares the physical fields computed by Endpoint-SD, Centered-SD, and direct finite differences under common RK4 time integration. With Centered-SD, we then study the error histories of three time integrators in Section 5.3 and the effect of conservation-based mesh adaptation in Section 5.4. Section 5.5 examines the evolution of independent initial perturbations within each spatial formulation.'))
change('section-5.1',0,para('We begin by specifying the initial fields and reference solutions used in the numerical experiments, together with the regions and error measures through which the computed fields are assessed. The four test problems cover a single soliton, interacting solitons, a localized Gaussian profile, and a periodic sinusoidal profile. These backgrounds allow us to examine the numerical formulations across different wave shapes and spatial distributions, while the continuous reference solutions provide a common basis for evaluating both physical fields. All numerical tests use $a=2$ and $\\lambda=-2$. For the one- and two-soliton cases, the reference solutions are generated directly from the continuous Gram determinant formulas (6)–(7) in Section 2.1, with $\\rho_i=1$ and zero phase shifts. Table 1 lists their spectral parameters, evaluation windows, and computational intervals.'))
change('section-5.1',-1,para('Here, $\\mathcal G_u$ and $\\mathcal G_v$ denote the native lattice sites of the two physical fields within the evaluation region. We also record the initial derivative errors $E_{u_t}$ and $E_{v_t}$, defined as the maximum absolute differences between the spatially computed and exact continuous time derivatives at $t=0$. These initial errors characterize the spatial approximation before time stepping, while $E_u(T)$ and $E_v(T)$ measure the resulting physical-field errors after evolution. Together, they allow us to examine how the initial spatial accuracy is reflected in the computed waves and how the two field components respond during the coupled evolution. In the tables below, bold entries indicate the smallest error among the compared methods for each case and field.'))
change('section-5.2',0,para('In this subsection, we examine how the spatial formulation affects the accuracy of the two physical fields, following the initial spatial approximation through to the computed wave profiles. The analysis in Section 3.4 identifies different truncation errors for Endpoint-SD and Centered-SD, and we use the four test problems to investigate how these differences are reflected in numerical evolution. Direct finite differences applied to the continuous DLW system provide a baseline. All three formulations use $h=\\Delta x=0.1$ and advance from the exact initial data to $T=0.01$ using classical RK4 with $\\Delta t=10^{-4}$. We first examine the initial time-derivative errors in Table 2, then consider the evolved physical-field errors and their spatial distributions.'))
change('section-5.2',[-2,-1],pars('The four tests show how the initial spatial errors are carried into the evolving physical fields. Endpoint-SD gives the smallest initial $u_t$ error in every case and retains the smallest terminal $u$ error for the Gaussian and sinusoidal backgrounds. In the soliton tests, Centered-SD gives smaller terminal errors in both fields than Endpoint-SD, while its $v$ error remains the smallest among all three formulations across the four cases. The initial derivative errors and the evolved field errors therefore reveal different aspects of the spatial approximation, whose influence develops through the coupled dynamics.',
'The spatial error analysis helps interpret this behavior. The lattice arrangement determines the leading truncation terms in each equation, while the coupling allows an error in one field to influence the evolution of the other. In particular, the $v_{xx}$ term provides a route through which the approximation of $v$ affects the computed $u$ field. These experiments thus connect the analytical properties of the semi-discrete formulations with the accuracy of the evolving waves, showing the importance of considering both physical fields together when assessing a spatial discretization.'))
change('section-5.3',0,para('In this subsection, we examine how time integration affects the development of physical-field errors for a fixed spatial discretization. The terminal errors describe the accuracy reached at the end of the computation, while their time histories show how the differences between integrators develop during evolution. We use Centered-SD on the uniform grid with $h=\\Delta x=0.1$ and compare forward Euler, classical RK4, and Crank–Nicolson. All calculations start from the same exact initial fields, use the same spatial operators and boundary data, and advance to $T=0.01$ with $\\Delta t=10^{-4}$. Following both field components allows us to examine how the temporal approximation combines with the spatial errors already present in the evolution.'))
change('section-5.3',-1,pars('The terminal errors and their time histories show that the choice of time integrator affects the two physical fields differently. At the tested time step, RK4 and Crank–Nicolson give closely matching error histories, while forward Euler produces a smaller $u$ error and a larger $v$ error throughout the computed interval. Reducing the Euler time step moves both errors toward the RK4 values, supporting the interpretation that temporal and spatial errors partially cancel in $u$ and reinforce one another in $v$.',
'These results help explain how time integration modifies the accuracy obtained from a given spatial formulation. The similar shapes of the error histories suggest that the spatial approximation and wave evolution largely determine the observed pattern of accumulation, while the temporal approximation changes its magnitude in each component. Following both fields over time makes this interaction visible and provides a fuller account of the computed accuracy than the terminal errors alone.'))
change('section-5.4',0,pars('In this subsection, we use the semi-discrete conservation law to examine how the distribution of spatial resolution affects the accuracy of Centered-SD. The soliton fields concentrate their variation along localized wave bands, and the conservation density provides a means of placing additional nodes in these regions. We compare uniform and adaptive meshes for the one- and two-soliton problems, following both the node distribution and the resulting physical-field errors. This allows us to examine how a conserved quantity from the semi-discrete system can guide the numerical resolution of its waves.',
'Both calculations use the same number of shared $x$ nodes, the same uniform transverse lattice with $h=0.1$, and RK4 with $\\Delta t=10^{-4}$ up to $T=0.01$. The uniform $x$ spacing is $\\Delta x=0.1$. On both meshes, the $x$ derivatives are evaluated using the mapped three-point centered difference operators of Section 4.3, so that the comparison uses a common spatial approximation while varying the node distribution. The soliton backgrounds provide a positive transverse-averaged conservation density for the equidistribution construction.'))
change('section-5.4',-1,pars('The mesh experiments show that the semi-discrete conservation density provides a useful basis for allocating spatial resolution. Equidistribution concentrates nodes along the soliton wave bands, where the uniform-grid errors are also largest, and reduces the errors in both physical fields at the same node count. The spatial error profiles further show that this improvement extends across the wave region, connecting the change in node placement with the location of the computed errors.',
'Over the present time interval, the node distribution changes only slightly, and the calculation with a fixed, initially adapted mesh gives similar error reductions. The main benefit therefore comes from the resolution pattern established by the conservation density. These results give the conservation law a concrete role in numerical computation: it guides the placement of nodes in a way that improves the approximation of the physical fields. The mesh construction thus provides a further connection between the analytical structure of the semi-discrete system and the accurate computation of its waves.'))
change('section-5.5',-1,para('These experiments show how a small change in one initial physical field develops through the coupled numerical evolution. Within each formulation, the initially perturbed component remains close to its original disturbance magnitude over the computed interval, while the other field acquires a response whose magnitude depends on the background and the perturbed component. The substantial induced $u$ response in the two-soliton case is consistent with the transverse integration in the first field equation, through which the influence of a $v$ perturbation can accumulate across the domain. Following the two inputs separately therefore helps us understand both the persistence of an initial disturbance and the response generated by the coupling. Alongside the preceding accuracy tests, these histories describe how the computed waves respond to changes in their physical initial data.'))
change('section-6',range(3),pars(
'In this work, we have developed two integrable semi-discretizations of the $(2+1)$-dimensional DLW system, connecting their bilinear construction, exact solutions, and nonlinear physical-field equations. A Miwa expansion of the Hirota–Miwa equation gives the endpoint formulation, Endpoint-SD. Starting from this lattice, a half-step shift of the parameter reference and a staggered placement of the $\\tau$ functions lead to the centered formulation, Centered-SD. We have constructed explicit Gram determinant solutions for both systems and identified common positivity conditions under which the logarithmic field reconstructions remain regular. The nonlinear equations are obtained from the corresponding bilinear relations, and their Darboux–Lax representations recover the evolution equations through compatibility. These results place the two formulations within a shared integrable framework, in which the lattice equations, determinant solutions, and reconstructed physical fields can be examined together.',
'Within this framework, the continuum analysis establishes how the two lattice arrangements approximate the continuous DLW system. Endpoint-SD has second-order consistency in the first physical-field equation and first-order consistency in the second, while the symmetric staggered construction of Centered-SD gives second-order consistency in both. We have also compared the corresponding exact Gram wave families with their continuous counterparts under suitable regularity and positivity assumptions, establishing their convergence orders and deriving explicit leading error coefficients for the physical fields. In particular, the Centered-SD fields converge at second order. The equation-level expansions describe how the continuous dynamics are recovered, while the exact-field estimates quantify how the wave profiles themselves change with the lattice spacing. Together, they show how different approximation orders and error coefficients can arise within a common integrable construction, and clarify the role of lattice placement and nonlinear field reconstruction in relating discrete wave families to continuous ones.',
'The numerical experiments show how these approximation properties develop during computation. The initial derivative errors distinguish the spatial approximations before time stepping, while the evolved field errors reveal their subsequent behavior under the coupled dynamics. Endpoint-SD gives the smallest initial $u_t$ errors in the four tests and the smallest terminal $u$ errors on the Gaussian and sinusoidal backgrounds. Centered-SD gives the smallest $v$ errors throughout these tests and smaller terminal errors than Endpoint-SD in both fields for the soliton problems. The time-integration experiments further show how temporal and spatial errors can combine differently in the two components. At the tested step size, RK4 and Crank–Nicolson produce closely matching histories, while the response to reducing the Euler time step supports partial error cancellation in $u$ and reinforcement in $v$. These observations connect the local approximation analysis with the accuracy of the evolving physical fields.',
'The semi-discrete conservation law also provides a constructive link between the analytical formulation and numerical resolution. Using its density to distribute the shared longitudinal nodes concentrates resolution along the soliton wave bands and reduces the errors in both fields at a fixed node count. Over the computed interval, the improvement comes mainly from the initial density-adapted node placement, illustrating how information intrinsic to the equations can guide the allocation of computational resolution. Independent perturbations of the initial fields complement these accuracy tests by revealing how a disturbance persists in one component and generates a response in the other. They provide a further view of the coupled numerical evolution through its dependence on the physical initial data.',
'Taken together, this study connects the construction of an integrable lattice with the interpretation and improvement of its physical-field computations. The exact solutions make approximation errors accessible to analysis, the field reconstruction exposes the influence of lattice placement, and the conservation law supplies information for adapting the mesh. Their combined use gives the analytical structure a practical role in understanding how numerical waves are resolved and how their errors develop. More broadly, the DLW system illustrates an approach in which integrable constructions and numerical analysis inform one another, with the physical fields providing the common point of reference. This perspective offers a basis for further studies of how integrable structure can support accurate computation in coupled, higher-dimensional wave systems.'))

js="const fs=require('fs'),k=require(process.argv[1]);process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(0,'utf8')).map(tex=>k.renderToString(tex,{throwOnError:true}))));"
r=subprocess.run(['node','-e',js,str(ROOT/'Workspaces/gsg_project/dlw_report/_assets/package/dist/katex.js')],input=json.dumps(maths),capture_output=True,text=True,encoding='utf-8',check=True)
rendered=json.loads(r.stdout);doc=re.sub(r'ACCEPTEDMATH(\d+)END',lambda m:rendered[int(m[1])],doc)
before=BeautifulSoup(original,'html.parser');after=BeautifulSoup(doc,'html.parser')
for selector in ['figure','table','div.equation']:
    assert [str(x) for x in before.select(selector)]==[str(x) for x in after.select(selector)],selector
for sid in ['section-2','section-3']:
    assert str(before.find(id=sid))==str(after.find(id=sid)),sid
assert not after.select('.katex-error')
assert 'ACCEPTEDMATH' not in doc
ids=[x['id'] for x in after.select('[id]')];assert len(ids)==len(set(ids))
assert not [a['href'] for a in after.select('a[href^="#"]') if a['href'][1:] not in ids]
assert len(after.find_all('figure'))==14 and len(after.find_all('table'))==7
P.write_bytes(doc.encode('utf-8'))
(HERE/'validation.json').write_text(json.dumps({'edits':edits,'backup':str(backup),'figures':14,'tables':7,'figures_tables_display_equations_unchanged':True,'sections_2_3_unchanged':True,'katex_errors':0,'internal_links':'passed','sha256':hashlib.sha256(P.read_bytes()).hexdigest()},indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
print('Accepted prose synchronized; all figures, tables and display equations preserved.')
